is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically the rule set applied to its WAN-facing interface eth2 (10.50.1.2/24, connected to router3 via the r6_r3 link).

**Mechanism:** Router6 serves as the gateway for two Location C subnets — 172.18.50.0/24 (eth0) and 172.18.51.0/24 (eth1). All traffic reaching these networks from the rest of the topology must cross router6's eth2 inbound. The FORWARD chain has three DROP rules covering eth2 inbound: one for TCP, one for UDP, and a catch-all for everything else (ICMP, etc.). These are preceded by a single narrow ACCEPT exception: TCP port 80 from source subnet 172.16.10.0/24 to destination 172.18.51.1 only. The 172.16.20.0/24 subnet (laptop0's home) is absent from any ACCEPT rule. Rule 4 (the catch-all DROP) already shows 20 matched packets, confirming active blocking.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, inbound on eth2.

**Root cause:** An iptables ACL on router6 grants only the 172.16.10.0/24 subnet a narrow HTTP exception through the WAN interface while dropping all other forwarded traffic — effectively locking laptop0 (172.16.20.10) out of the 172.18.51.x and 172.18.50.x networks while leaving pc4/pc5 (172.16.10.x) with privileged access.

**Fix:** On router6, either remove the blanket DROP rules (`iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, same for udp and the catch-all) and replace them with appropriate per-source policy, or insert an ACCEPT rule for 172.16.20.0/24 before the DROP rules so laptop0 can reach the Location C subnets.
=== END ===